You are building a production-grade Trivia application.

This application contains two bounded modules:

1. Trivia Runtime
2. Trivia Authoring/Admin

You must follow these global rules at all times.

---

# ARCHITECTURE RULES (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

## 1. STRICT SERVICE BOUNDARIES

This application is:
- the Trivia Runtime system
- the Trivia Authoring/Admin system

It is NOT:
- the platform
- the lead system
- the rewards system
- the redemption system
- the campaign management system

NEVER:
- store leads as the source of truth
- manage rewards
- implement leaderboard ownership
- absorb campaign ownership from the platform
- move platform responsibilities into Trivia

If something belongs to another system, integrate through API or reference only. Do not implement it here.

---

## 2. TWO INTERNAL MODULES, ONE APP

This app has two distinct internal surfaces:

### Runtime
Owns:
- session lifecycle
- question delivery
- answer validation
- timers
- scoring
- completion handling
- runtime snapshots

### Authoring/Admin
Owns:
- quiz shells
- question bank
- CSV import
- webhook ingestion
- review and approval
- shell theme/screen configuration
- shell validation
- shell visibility rules

Do not blur Runtime and Authoring responsibilities.

---

## 3. SINGLE RESPONSIBILITY

Every module must have ONE clear responsibility:

- controllers / routes → handle HTTP
- services → handle business logic
- repositories / data layer → handle database access
- validators → handle input/config validation
- mappers / serializers → shape response payloads

Do NOT mix these layers.

---

## 4. NO SHORTCUTS / NO MOCK LOGIC IN FINAL CODE

- No fake data in production paths
- No hardcoded IDs
- No temporary logic left behind
- All configs must come from DB or validated input
- No demo-only assumptions inside production flows

---

# DATABASE RULES

## 1. KEYS AND TIMESTAMPS
- Use UUIDs for all primary keys
- Always include created_at timestamps
- Include updated_at where records are edited over time

## 2. RELATIONSHIPS
- Use proper foreign key relationships
- Preserve referential integrity
- Add indexes for common filters and joins

## 3. SNAPSHOT RULE
- Runtime sessions must store immutable snapshots of resolved config and selected questions
- Runtime must not depend on live mutable shell state once play begins

## 4. REVIEW / AUDIT SUPPORT
- Persist review state where needed
- Persist import batch history
- Persist webhook logs
- Persist shell status transitions where applicable

## 5. DERIVED VALUES
- Do not store derived values unless needed for performance, auditability, or runtime stability

---

# API DESIGN RULES

## 1. CONSISTENT ROUTING
Keep route naming RESTful and consistent.

Runtime examples:
- POST /trivia/start
- GET /trivia/{id}/next
- POST /trivia/{id}/answer
- POST /trivia/{id}/complete

Authoring examples should also be consistent and resource-oriented.

## 2. RESPONSE SHAPE
Always return structured JSON:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {},
  "error": null
}

Errors:

{
  "success": false,
  "error": {
    "code": "ERROR_CODE",
    "message": "Human readable message"
  }
}

## 3. NEVER RETURN RAW DATABASE OBJECTS
Map responses intentionally.

---

# RUNTIME RULES

## 1. SESSION IS SOURCE OF TRUTH
- All gameplay must be driven from server-side session state
- Never trust frontend state

## 2. IMMUTABLE QUESTION SET
Once a session starts:
- question set must not change
- resolved config must not change for that session
- gameplay runs from snapshot only

## 3. ANSWER VALIDATION
- Must be server-side only
- Never trust client-submitted correctness

## 4. TIMER ENFORCEMENT
- Must be enforced server-side
- Frontend timer is only visual

## 5. FULL-SET RESOLUTION
For randomized play:
- build the full question set before gameplay proceeds
- do not select questions one-by-one during play

---

# AUTHORING / ADMIN RULES

## 1. SHELL-FIRST MODEL
A shell is the reusable base template for a trivia experience.

Authoring owns:
- shell metadata
- shell defaults
- shell theme/screen config
- fixed question attachment
- validation state

Do not let campaign overrides mutate the shell itself.

## 2. QUESTION BANK MODEL
Questions live in a reusable bank.
Questions may be attached to shells, but do not belong only to a single shell by default.

## 3. IMPORT MODEL
CSV and webhook imports must:
- validate input
- create import logs/batches
- land questions in review state
- avoid auto-publishing

## 4. REVIEW MODEL
Imported questions must support review/approval workflow before use.

## 5. MOBILE-FIRST PRESENTATION
Do not build a freeform drag-and-drop page builder.
Use a constrained theme/config model.

---

